\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces {\bf  Entropy as Expected Uncertainty}   Entropy $H(p)$, or the expected uncertainty of a binary outcome, is plotted as a function of the event probability $p$. The curve is maximized at $p = 0.5$, where uncertainty is highest. As $p\to 0$ or $p \to 1$, the outcome becomes more predictable and the entropy approaches 0\cite  {shannonMathematicalTheoryCommunication,saraivaShannonEntropyIts2023}.}}{10}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:entropy_graph}{{6}{10}{{\bf Entropy as Expected Uncertainty} \\ Entropy $H(p)$, or the expected uncertainty of a binary outcome, is plotted as a function of the event probability $p$. The curve is maximized at $p = 0.5$, where uncertainty is highest. As $p\to 0$ or $p \to 1$, the outcome becomes more predictable and the entropy approaches 0\cite {shannonMathematicalTheoryCommunication,saraivaShannonEntropyIts2023}}{figure.caption.12}{}}
